    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The word Puritan began as a term of abuse; those so called spoke of themselves as &quot;the godly&quot;, &quot;saints&quot;, &quot;professors&quot; or &quot;God's children&quot;.</li><li>Puritans were dissatisfied with the limited extent of the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and divided over church government between supporters of episcopal, presbyterian and congregational types.</li><li>Puritans came to power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-english-civil-war">First English Civil War</a> but never won the religious settlement they wanted; around 2,400 clergy left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> in the Great Ejection of 1662.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Around 21,000 people crossed the Atlantic in the Puritan migration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, which ceased by 1641; their descendants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> number more than 16 million.</p></aside>
    </section>

